// A WHOLE-VALUE write through an `exclusive` borrow must land in the CALLER's
// object, the same way a field write must.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM write_through.cell. That file measures
// which ADDRESS a call site hands over, and its mutator is deliberately
// bodyless in C, for the reason its own header gives. So it exercises exactly
// one of the two shapes an `exclusive` borrow can be written through:
//
//     b.len = b.len + 1        the FIELD form, which write_through.cell's C
//                              host performs, and which the MLIR backend
//                              refuses in a Cell body to this day
//                              ("assignment through a field path")
//     b = Buffer { ... }       the WHOLE-VALUE form, which no example used at
//                              all, and which the MLIR backend silently
//                              MISCOMPILED
//
// The gate ran nine stages green over that gap. Stage 8 compares the backends
// on their ANSWERS rather than their verdicts, which is exactly the stage that
// would have caught this, and it could not, because the shape was absent from
// the corpus. A comparison is only as good as the programs it compares, so the
// fix for a silent class of defect is never only the compiler change: the
// corpus has to carry the shape, or the next regression is silent again.
//
// WHAT WENT WRONG, measured. A borrowed struct parameter's slot holds the
// CALLER's address rather than the object. The whole-value assignment stored
// the new struct INTO that slot, overwriting the pointer, so the callee wrote
// over its own local copy of the address and the caller's object never
// changed. `cell check` accepted it, all three backends emitted it, mlir-opt
// verified it, it linked and it ran:
//
//     C backend     42        correct
//     LLVM backend  42        correct, since 8b82b62
//     MLIR backend  WRONG, and silent, in two different ways depending on the
//                   struct's width. A one-field Buffer stores 8 bytes into an
//                   8-byte pointer slot, fits, and prints 37: the write is
//                   merely lost. THIS file's two-field Buffer stores 16 bytes
//                   into that same 8-byte slot, so it also overflows it, and
//                   this exact program printed 1 (reproducibly, three runs).
//
// WHAT IT PRINTS, and how to read a wrong answer. One integer, `42`:
//
//   `buf.len` starts at 37 and each of the five unique-borrow spellings adds
//   `buf.step`, which is 1. So 42 means all five wrote through and 41 means
//   exactly one lost its write, which is the reading that matters for a
//   REGRESSION: a call site that passes a copy for some spellings and the
//   caller's object for others lands between 37 and 42. Anything else, 1
//   included, means the callee wrote somewhere it should not have.
//
// WHY `step` IS A SECOND FIELD RATHER THAN THE LITERAL 1. The whole-value
// write has to copy every field, and reading `b.step` forces the callee to
// index the borrowed object at an offset PAST THE FIRST on the way in as well
// as on the way out. A one-field struct would let a backend that indexes the
// slot instead of loading through it still produce 42 by accident, because at
// offset 0 a wrong base and a right base can coincide.
//
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 42

pub struct Buffer {
    copy len: Int,
    copy step: Int,
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

// The mutator has a Cell BODY here, unlike write_through.cell's `grow`, and
// that is the whole point: what is under test is what the CALLEE emits for
// `b = Buffer { ... }`, which no C host can stand in for. All three backends
// lower this body, because the assignment target is the binding itself and
// not a field path.
pub fn bump(exclusive b: Buffer) {
    b = Buffer { len: b.len + b.step, step: b.step }
}

pub fn main() {
    var owned buf = Buffer { len: 37, step: 1 }

    // All five unique-borrow spellings, which examples/borrows.cell declares
    // identical and which do NOT reach a backend as one shape: `exclusive
    // buf` arrives as a bare reference because `hir.lower` consumes the
    // written prefix, while the four sigil forms arrive wrapped in a unary
    // borrow node. A backend that handles one and not the others splits the
    // group silently, and this arithmetic is what makes that visible.
    bump(exclusive buf)
    bump(&mut buf)
    bump(&var buf)
    bump(&exclusive buf)
    bump(exclusive &buf)

    print_int(buf.len)
}
